= Morita Equivalence <ch:morita-equivalence>

#source(163)We shall need to study the relationship between $Lambda$-modules and
$M_n (Lambda)$-modules, where as usual $M_n (Lambda)$ is the ring of all
$n times n$ matrices over the ring $Lambda$. The generalization of this
relationship leads to the concept of Morita equivalence, and we sketch here some
of the main results of this theory. As references, we cite Bass [@bib:Bass1968],
P.~M. Cohn [@bib:Cohn1966], Faith [@bib:Faith1973]. While the general theory
holds for abelian categories, we shall make the simplifying assumption that our
categories are in fact categories of modules. Denote by $cal(M)_Lambda$ the
category of right $Lambda$-modules, by $lmodule(Delta, cal(M))_Lambda$ the
category of $(Delta, Lambda)$-bimodules, and so on.
